  "summary": "Imperial architectural style of the Nguyễn Dynasty characterized by elaborate ornamentation, curved roofs, and Chinese-inspired elements.",
  "description": "Nguyễn architecture features intricate wood carvings, ceramic roof decorations, and symmetrical layouts reflecting the dynasty's power and cosmopolitan influences. Its design represents the pinnacle of Vietnamese imperial court architecture with distinctive regional adaptations.",
  "culturalContext": "Nguyễn Dynasty architecture (1802-1945) — Hue's imperial citadel, royal tombs (Tu Duc, Khai Dinh) and the Forbidden Purple City — fused Vietnamese timber carpentry with Qing Chinese axial planning and French colonial touches: purple-glazed tile roofs, dragon-cornice ironwood frames, inlaid mother-of-pearl interiors and Khai Dinh's mosaic-frenzied concrete-vented tomb. UNESCO-listed Complex of Hue Monuments survives war and typhoon as Vietnam's imperial memory palace.",
